challenges.description:
The CodeDev grading, email and certificate workers write one log line per job event, and the on-call team wants a per-minute lag report built from that log instead of a depth chart. Write queueLag(sloSeconds, lines), and the helpers parseLine and nearestRank, in main.ts.

Input (stdin, JSON): { "sloSeconds": number, "lines": string[] }. Each line is "<timestamp> <type> <queue> <jobId>", fields separated by whitespace (leading, trailing and repeated spaces are allowed). The timestamp is UTC with whole seconds, exactly YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ, and must be a real date and time (2026-02-30T09:00:00Z is not). The type is enqueue, dequeue or complete. Every other line - wrong number of fields, unknown type, bad timestamp, empty - is skipped and counted in skippedLines.

Jobs: a job is identified by queue + jobId. Lines can be out of order and can repeat (log shipping duplicates, a redelivered job dequeued twice): for each job keep the earliest enqueue, the earliest dequeue and the earliest complete. A job with no enqueue line was enqueued before the log starts: ignore it entirely.

Windows: one window per queue per UTC minute, for every queue named in a well-formed line and every minute from the minute of the earliest well-formed line to the minute of the latest one (all queues share this range). For the window [start, end), end = start + 60 s:
- depth: jobs waiting at end - enqueued before end, with no dequeue before end and no complete before end (a job that is being processed is not waiting; a job with a complete line but a lost dequeue line is not waiting after its complete);
- oldestAgeSec: the largest end - enqueue among those jobs, in seconds; 0 when depth is 0;
- waitP50Sec, waitP95Sec: percentiles of dequeue - enqueue for jobs whose dequeue is in [start, end); null when there are none;
- processingP95Sec: p95 of complete - dequeue for jobs whose complete is in [start, end) and that have a dequeue; null when there are none;
- breach: oldestAgeSec > sloSeconds.
Percentiles use the nearest-rank method: sort the n values ascending and take the value at 1-based rank ceil(p / 100 * n). For 20 values, p50 is the 10th and p95 the 19th.

Output (stdout, printed by the runner): { "skippedLines": number, "windows": [{ "queue", "minute": "YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM", "depth", "oldestAgeSec", "waitP50Sec", "waitP95Sec", "processingP95Sec", "breach" }] }, sorted by queue name (plain string comparison) and then by minute. With no well-formed line, windows is []. Logs have at most a few thousand lines; standard library only.
